The solution f (t, x, v) represents the density of bacteria at any given time t for any location x moving with velocity v∈ V . The two terms describe different aspects of the motion. The v • ∇ x f term characterizes the "run"-part: bacteria move in a straight line with velocity v, and the terms on the right characterize the "tumble"-part: bacteria change from having velocity v ′ to v using the transitional rate K(x, v, v ′ ) ≥ 0. This transition rate thus is termed the tumbling kernel.
